<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramanathaswamy Temple</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ramanathaswamy Temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a> island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, one of the twelve jyotirlingas and a major pilgrimage site linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>'s return from Lanka. Its present structure was largely built from the 12th century onward, and the Sethupathi rulers of Ramnad added its famous pillared corridors in the 17th and 18th centuries, among the longest of any Hindu temple.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramanathaswamy-temple.jpg" alt="Ramanathaswamy Temple" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ssriram mt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramanathaswamy_temple7.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
